  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "_dwarf_create_cie_from_after_start",
    "file": "libdwarf/src/lib/libdwarf/dwarf_frame2.c",
    "description": "In the `aug_irix_exception_table` (`\"z\"` augmentation in `.debug_frame`) path, validation of the decoded `length_of_augmented_fields` is backwards: line 891 rejects values smaller than `dbg->de_filesize` but accepts attacker-controlled huge ULEB128 values. The subsequent unchecked `frame_ptr += length_of_augmented_fields` can wrap to an address below the section. The code only checks `frame_ptr > section_ptr_end`, so the wrapped pointer is stored as `ci_cie_instr_start`. Later `_dwarf_get_fde_info_for_a_pc_row` derives an apparently ordered instruction end from this invalid pointer and `_dwarf_exec_frame_instr` crashes when reading the first instruction byte. The parser must validate the augmentation length against the bytes remaining in the CIE/section before pointer addition and detect both overflow and pointers below the section start.",
    "conditions": "A crafted object supplies a `.debug_frame` CIE with augmentation string `\"z\"`, selecting `aug_irix_exception_table`, and an oversized ULEB128 augmented-fields length. The value must be large enough to evade the reversed `< dbg->de_filesize` check and cause pointer addition to wrap below the mapped frame section. An associated FDE must then be queried for register-row information; the harness reaches this through `dwarf_get_fde_list`, `print_fde_selected_regs`, and `dwarf_get_fde_info_for_reg3_b`, which initializes the CIE table and dereferences the corrupted instruction pointer."
  },
